# The in-language prelude: words written in this language rather than as Rust
# primitives, parsed and evaluated into the global frame when an engine starts.
#
# This is V6's "vocabulary, mostly in-language" (`direction-v2.md`). What lands
# here is everything *definable* over the primitive core — so the Rust tables
# shrink to the words that genuinely cannot be written down: the ones that touch
# the stack machine, the environment, or the float unit. The derived stack
# shuffles (`over rot nip tuck dupd 2dup 2drop`), the combinators (`dip keep bi`),
# and the flow words (`when unless cond`) all belong here eventually.

# `each` ( lst f -- ... ) — apply `f` to each element of `lst`, in order.
#
# **The one iteration word.** `map`, `flatMap`, and `reduce` are not separate
# words but calling conventions on this one, because a function may leave any
# number of values and `[ ]` is an ordinary runtime mark:
#
#     lst &f each              forEach     f : 1 -> 0
#     [ lst &f each ]          map         f : 1 -> 1
#     [ lst &f each ]          flatMap     f : 1 -> n    — the same code
#     seed lst &f each         reduce      f : 2 -> 1    — the stack accumulates
#
# Map and flatMap coincide because there is no intermediate container to
# flatten. Reduce needs no accumulator parameter because the seed simply sits
# below the working area. A `map` that opened its own region would be strictly
# *less* expressive than `[ … each ]`, which is why it is absent: welding
# collection to iteration gives up the mark's defining property, that it is an
# ordinary stack value you can reach back over.
#
# **Written over `length`/`nth`, not `first`/`rest`.** `rest` is O(n) here — the
# list is bound, so `Rc::make_mut` clones it every step — which makes the obvious
# cons-style definition quadratic: measured 160 ms -> 2.8 s going from n=2000 to
# n=16000, against 52 ms -> 201 ms for this index form. It is also what makes
# `each` generic the moment V5 gives strings and dicts those two attributes,
# where a native version would need an arm per type.
#
# **`step` recurses in tail position**, so the evaluation loop replaces its
# activation instead of nesting: iteration depth is bounded by memory, not by
# the Rust stack, and a list of any length runs flat. `f` is applied *before*
# the tail call, so it gets an ordinary nested activation and may itself be
# anything callable — a function, a builtin, or a plain value.
'each { lst f:
    'step { i:
        i lst length <
        { lst i nth f   i 1 + step }
        { }
        if
    } =
    0 step
} =
